  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Widely scattered showers diminish early this \nevening...otherwise dry and cool tonight with lows between 35 and 45 \ndegrees.\n\nThe departing shortwave and loss of diurnal heating will bring an \nend to the widely scattered showers early this evening. \nOtherwise...a ridge of high pressure moves off the mid-Atlantic \ncoast tonight. This will result in a period of clearing with good \nradiational cooling conditions for the much of the night. This \nshould allow low temps to bottom out mainly in the 35 to 45 degree \nrange with the coolest of those readings in the outlying locations \nof northwest MA. Shortwave energy will be approaching from the west \nlate. This will result in increasing cloudiness after midnight from \nthe southwest...but still expect enough of a window for good \nradiational cooling conditions. We may see a few showers develop \ntoward daybreak as warm advection aloft commences.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Scattered showers Sat morning become widespread \nduring the afternoon/early evening hours from west to east. High \ntemps Sat mainly in the middle 50s to the lower 60s.\n\nAs the upper trough digs into the Great Lakes...it will result in a \nwarm front lifting north of the region Sat morning. This forcing \ncombined with a developing southerly LLJ will result in some showers \ndeveloping Sat morning. The showers will be scattered in \nnature...but the widespread heavier showers will overspread the \nregion during the afternoon/early evening as the main shortwave/pre-\nfrontal trough approaches the region. This will strengthen the \nsoutherly LLJ further and there is a bit of elevated instability as \nwell. So some brief heavy downpours and perhaps an embedded t-storm \nor two later Sat into Sat evening. Greatest risk of an isolated t-\nstorm or two will be across RI/SE MA on the nose of the LLJ...But \nnot expecting a widespread threat. We should see the bulk of the \nsteady rain exit the coast by late Sat evening...but a few showers \nalong with low clouds and patchy fog will persist. \n\nThe clouds, onshore flow and arriving rain will hold high temps on \nSaturday in the middle 50s to the lower 60s. Low temps Sat night \nwill only drop into the upper 40s and lower 50s with low clouds and \nfog patches persisting. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Increasing confidence in beneficial rainfall Sunday \nevening into Monday followed by a dry period through mid-week.\n\nExpecting another mid-level trough with an embedded shortwave to \npush through southern New England Sunday evening into Monday \nmorning. The latest suite of guidance is indicating a surge of \nwarm, moist air ahead of a cold front Sunday - bringing afternoon \ntemperatures possibly into the low 70s for most areas, but also \nincreasing PWATs upwards of 1.0\" or so. This supports beneficial \nrainfall through Sunday night and into Monday morning before a \nsignificantly cooler and drier airmass moves into place later \nMonday, with high temperatures slowly increasing from the mid-50s \nMonday to the low 60s by Wednesday.\n\nEnsemble guidance is indicating another possible round of showers \nmoving through southern New England sometime on Thursday, but \ndetails remain uncertain given the longer time frame.",
